# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# v4.2 FIX: do not let constants propagate across the block boundary.
#
# `weight_rd_data[29:0]` is driven in the RTL by
#     assign weight_rd_data[N] = 1'b0;
# (the paper-aligned core does not implement the weight read-back bus -- the host
# gets the weights from the w_wr broadcast stream instead). Without this setting
# DC propagates that constant THROUGH the output port, so the port ends up with
# NO DRIVER AT ALL, no net, and therefore no pin. FC then reports, during
# place_opt:
#     Warning: The port named 'weight_rd_data[0]' cannot be isolated because it
#              is not driven (OPT-4016)
#     Isolated 0 ports, skipped 30 ports
# and `set_isolate_ports` refuses to touch an undriven port, which is why the
# earlier attempt reported ISO_OK and changed nothing.
#
# The consequence at sign-off: the routed GDS carries 204 port texts against a
# 232-port netlist, so Calibre LVS reports "Different numbers of ports" and the
# macro could not have been integrated -- a 30-bit port with 30 pins missing.
#
# With boundary optimisation OFF, DC must instantiate a real tie cell to drive
# each such port, so every port has a driver, a net and a pin. This is also the
# CORRECT setting for a block that will be instantiated by something else:
# boundary optimisation is only safe when the block is the whole chip, whose
# boundary is the package rather than a parent design.
set_boundary_optimization $DESIGN false
